{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310839,"nonce":"39K+9ZSfB0zF1csbF8Fcxn8T55c=","title":"Phase 2 of broadening worker_pool coverage beyond qmatmul (extending parallel_rows_no_nest to ...","message":"279-line patch on `runtime/src/tensor.rs` (230+/49-) routing six kernel\ngroups through `worker_pool::parallel_rows_no_nest`:\n\n- new `dispatch_binop_parallel` helper at L1474; add/sub/mul/div route through\n  it with threshold `numel \u003e= 16384 \u0026\u0026 pool.workers() \u003e 1`\n- `tensor_unary` F32 fast path (drives silu/gelu/exp/log/sqrt) at L1757 with\n  threshold `numel \u003e= 8192`\n- `rayzor_tensor_relu` F32 path at L1828 with `numel \u003e= 16384`\n- `rayzor_tensor_rope` at L2130 parallelised over flat `seq_len*num_heads`\n  with `total_blocks * half \u003e= 4096 \u0026\u0026 total_blocks \u003e= 2`; threads clamped\n  to `pool.workers().min(total_blocks)`\n- `rayzor_tensor_gather_rows` at L2584 with `k*row_bytes \u003e= 4096 \u0026\u0026 k \u003e= 2`\n\nAll call sites use `parallel_rows_no_nest` so reentrant pool use is safe by\nconstruction. Build was clean (54s, no new warnings).\n\n\nThree independent re-measurements on current HEAD (327fd80) showed Phase 1\ndoes not move the wall:\n\n| measurement | n   | mean delta tok/s | Welch p | verdict |\n|-------------|-----|------------------|---------|---------|\n| audit phase-2 (vs stale a8cef3c baseline) | 5x5 | -2.94% | 0.0004  | regression |\n| measurement-validity replicate (current HEAD) | 5x5 | +2.80% | 0.125 | noise |\n| correctness replicate (current HEAD, 10x10) | 10x10 | -9.29% mean / -3.97% median | 0.043 | regression |\n\nZERO measurements reached the green-ship bar (`p \u003c= 0.05` AND `\u003e= 5%`\nspeedup). Audit's red verdict was right on direction (regression) even\nthough its 1% CV baseline appears to have been thermally-favourable\n(replicates see CV 3-22% depending on system load).\n\nStability angle: audit reported 2/10 SIGSEGV/SIGTRAP at\n`safety::tests::test_misaligned_pointer_is_invalid` exit; two later\nreplicates couldn't reproduce in 5+5 runs. Inconclusive — but unnecessary\nto land scaffolding that doesn't help perf.\n\nCorrectness held throughout: llama-chat output is byte-for-byte identical\nto llama.cpp on the Paris/colours prompts, and pre-existing divergences\non haiku/2+2 reproduce identically on baseline (not Phase 1 artefacts).\n\n\nDecode-step tensor sizes on Llama 3.2 1B:\n- silu input: `[1, 8192]` (FFN gate), fires 16x/token at ~5-10 us each\n- mul (gate*up): `[1, 8192]`, fires 16x/token\n- RoPE Q: `numel=2048` (32 heads * 64), 16x/token; K: `numel=512`, 16x/token\n- add/sub residual: `[1, 2048]`, common throughout\n\nFor each of these, per-call work on a single P-core is ~5-30 microseconds.\n`parallel_rows_no_nest` enqueue + wake on M1 Pro is in the same order of\nmagnitude (the pool uses condvar wake; even with QOS_USER_INITIATED\nbiasing, P-core wake latency is several microseconds). When the dispatch\noverhead is similar to the kernel runtime, fork-join can break even at\nbest — and sub-millisecond kernels with cold-cache fan-out can go\nbackward, because each worker re-fetches the input slice.\n\nThe audit's break-even analysis underestimated wake cost by ~5-10x for\nthese sizes. Where parallel_rows_no_nest DOES win (qmatmul at\n`[hidden, ffn]`, prefill-shape silu/mul/rope), it was already there or\nnot exercised by the decode bench.\n\n\nBench at current HEAD: 0.85 tok/s, 14.3s wall for 24 tokens on Llama 3.2\n1B Q4_K_M / M1 Pro. Decode-step is ~600 ms.\n\n- qmatmul (Q4_K_M MT + SDOT): already parallel + SIMD. ~60-70% of step.\n- Haxe-side scalar loops (KVCache.append, attention mask build, sampler\n  argmax): single-threaded by construction. ~20-30% of step. Audit\n  flagged KVCache.append as next candidate but requires Haxe-side\n  rewrite (`rayzor.concurrent.WorkerPool.parallelRows`), not runtime.\n- Elementwise (silu/mul/add/rope/gather): ~5-10% of step. Phase 1\n  scope, but per-call too small to parallelise on M1 Pro decode.\n\nThe next real bottleneck is Haxe-side, not Rust-side, and importing\n`WorkerPool` from `nue.*` still cascades into trap-stubs (see\n`project_qmatmul_threaded.md` followup). That compiler bug needs fixing\nbefore Haxe-side parallelism can land.\n\n\n- Prefill-heavy workloads: silu/mul input becomes `[1, prompt_len, 8192]`\n  with `numel = prompt_len * 8192`. For prompt_len \u003e= 8 the threshold\n  fires and the work per worker is well above wake cost.\n- Long-context generation: RoPE work scales with seq_len; at decode\n  step seq_len=1, but at prefill / \u003e=128 ctx parallelism wins.\n- Larger models (Llama 7B/8B): hidden=4096, ffn=11008/14336; per-call\n  work is 2-4x the 1B case, moving the break-even into reach.\n\nIf those workloads become the focus, re-apply this patch with the\nexisting thresholds (they're correct for prefill — wrong for 1B\ndecode) and re-bench against the relevant workload, not the 24-token\ndecode bench used here.\n\n\n- `/Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/rayzor/runtime/src/tensor.rs` (now back\n  to HEAD 327fd80 via `git checkout --`)\n\n\n- [[project-qmatmul-threaded]] — the wins that DID land (SDOT + MT qmatmul)\n- [[project-addinto-fused-kernel]] — the in-place addInto fusion that\n  cut residual-add overhead and shifted the long pole away from\n  elementwise ops\n- [[project-llama-chat-first-bench]] — superseded baseline (0.22 tok/s);\n  current HEAD is 0.85 tok/s\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_kernel_parallelism_phase2_reverted` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}